<?nvs
// Shows how the output of `nvs config dump` matches the settings a program gets when it runs.

// `nvs config check nvs.toml` reads the file and runs nothing. It prints this line and exits with `0`:
//     ok: 1 file, 4 directives set, 0 overrides, 0 warnings

// `nvs config dump nvs.toml` prints every setting. These lines are the settings that the file sets:
//     app.0.limits.wall_time = "120s"
//     app.0.root             = "."
//     limits.memory          = "256M"
//     limits.wall_time       = "30s"
// Every other line is a default value, such as `log.target = "stderr"`. `--origin` adds the file, or `default`.

// This program is in the directory of block `app.0`, so its time limit is the block's value.
// This prints `time limit: 120s`.
echo 'time limit: ', Core\Config::get('limits.wall_time') ?? '(unset)', "\n";

// The block does not set `memory`, so the value from `[limits]` is used.
// This prints `memory:     256M`.
echo 'memory:     ', Core\Config::get('limits.memory') ?? '(unset)', "\n";
